alright let's take some questions from  the audience first question is from Jen  who says do you guys believe in the  butterfly effect chaos theory or karma  okay so let's address these one by one  if by karma you mean the sort of idea  that if you act in a way that is more  ethical you are going to sort of cause  better things to happen to you and that  if you act in ways that are unethical  you are sort of bringing on some kind of  future bad luck or negative  repercussions I generally don't believe  that pat in the literal sense although I  do believe that if you are someone who  tries to act ethically and help others  and and be moral that by virtue of that  you will probably have better  relationships and end up with more  opportunities and in more positions for  things to happen to you but that's not  really karma in the cosmic sense I don't  think no karma seems to be more of a  supernatural type of phenomenon I think  that people who don't identify as  religious but say that their spiritual  may be more likely to believe in this  kind of thing but for the most part I  just think it's coincidence I mean  coincidence happens all the time it's  all around us and I think sometimes  there's a more simple explanation for  these things that go on rather than  something supernatural like karma or the  butterfly effect okay so that's karma  now the butterfly effect there's debate  over whether it's supernatural or not  because the butterfly effect or  sometimes known as sort of chaos theory  is the idea that an action as small as a  butterfly flapping its wings in the  Amazon in Brazil for example causes a  sequence of events of growing and  growing sort of chain-linked magnitude  such that it could cause a hurricane on  the other side of the world okay and  there's a sort of scientific approach to  that which which which could seek to  answer the question generally speaking I  completely agree  that actions even small actions can have  consequences and some of those  consequences are sort of sequential in  the sense of a chain reaction that is  caused however many people who take two  they take this to the extreme in the  sort of butterfly example where a single  butterfly flapping its wings in the  Amazon creates a chain reaction of  events that builds into causing a  hurricane that otherwise would not have  happened on the other side of the world  it ignores sort of two aspects of  reality one is the cumulative effect of  events that take place on the planet so  if indeed the butterfly flapping its  wings had a role to play in a hurricane  that ultimately happened on the other  side of the world it could not really  have been that butterfly alone that  would have caused that but we might be  able to make a scientific case that it  was part of a cumulative series of  events that led to the hurricane  happening but there's another aspect  that's often ignored which is that  there's an underlying dampening ability  of our planet and the world that can  balance against any individual event so  as an example you could say okay i  rear-ended someone which caused that  person to be 10 minutes late getting to  work while we exchange insurance  information and because they were 10  minutes late they didn't interrupt a  funeral motorcade as a result of the  funeral motorcade not being interrupted  an attendee of the funeral was able to  more quickly get back to their office  and have a business phone call they  thought they weren't going to be able to  make and the decisions made on that  business phone call had such wide  reaching political implications that two  years later a war did not happen as a  result and thus me rear-ending someone  caused a war not to happen it sounds  very cool and interesting and sort of  metaphysically compelling the problem  though is that there is this dan  bending effect of going back to  equilibrium and at some point in that  hypothetical sequence that I lined up  the the sequential and growing impact of  the rear ending would be balanced out  because there was maybe 10 a buffer of  10 minutes already built in so that one  person getting delayed by 10 minutes  didn't end up having that impact or  something like that I don't know if I'm  sort of being cleared Pat with the idea  but when people talk about these these  butterfly effect hypotheses they ignore  the natural dampening effect of the real  world I mean I think this is certainly  true on a macro scale so to bring time  travel into this if we were to go back  in time and try to stop the  assassination of John F Kennedy there  would be so many world events that  happened thereafter that would  completely change the course of history  so if we're willing to accept that it  happens on a scale that big can we make  the same argument if we scale it down to  something as seemingly insignificant as  a butterfly flapping its wings I don't  know that we can I think it's kind of a  jump yeah although you know the the go  back in time one also gets into what's  called the grandmother paradox which is  if someone goes back and murders their  grandmother before she gave birth to  their mother could they have actually  gone back in time and done it because  they never would have been born because  they would have prevented their mother  from being born that's a whole other  thing that that is very interesting but  it's sort of a different interesting but  different question as well so big  picture conceptually I believe that  actions have consequences do I believe  in the butterfly effect as sort of  caricatured not really
 

